th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/limit-at-infinity.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/limit-at-infinity.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a limit at infinity describe?","options":["The value of $f$ at one very large but fixed finite input","The largest element of the domain","The behaviour of $f(x)$ as the input grows without bound","The derivative of $f$ at infinity"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"93a7511de2bba6bf48816baf","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What happens to $x^2$ as $x\\to-\\infty$?","options":["As $x$ becomes very negative, $x^2$ approaches $0$","As $x$ becomes very negative, $x^2$ grows without bound","As $x$ becomes very positive, $x^2$ approaches $-\\infty$","As $x$ approaches $0$, $x^2$ becomes infinite"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6c9d7b013218201b70232b14","time_limit":35},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_at_infinity_add_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\dfrac{1}{x}$?","options":["$0$","$1$","$+\\infty$","It does not exist"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9f99519395541288180fbe1a","time_limit":20},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_at_infinity_add_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"What is $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\dfrac{2x+1}{x}$?","options":["$2$","$1$","$0$","$+\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c9d0cff84749c20c424b471f","time_limit":30},{"id":"expansion_pack_043_limit_at_infinity_add_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A function satisfies $f(x)=3$ for all $x \\gt 10$ and $f(x)=-1$ for all $x \\lt -10$. Which statement follows?","options":["Both limits must equal $3$.","$\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}f(x)=3$ and $\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}f(x)=-1$.","Both limits must equal $-1$.","Neither limit can exist."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ee78a326404848bf242a43e2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_003_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Consider $f(x)=\\sin(\\pi x)$. Which statement is correct?","options":["$\\sin(\\pi n)\\to0$ as $n\\to\\infty$, but $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\sin(\\pi x)$ does not exist.","Since $\\sin(\\pi n)=0$ for every integer $n$, $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\sin(\\pi x)=0$.","$\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}\\sin(\\pi x)$ exists, but the sequence $\\sin(\\pi n)$ has no limit.","Neither $\\sin(\\pi n)$ nor $\\sin(\\pi x)$ has a limit, since sine oscillates."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7bde91d60325c86b75391700","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_005_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which assumption is part of the definition of $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}f(x)=L$ for $f\\colon X\\to\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["There is $M_0\\in\\mathbb{R}$ with $(M_0,+\\infty)\\subseteq X$.","$X$ is unbounded above, i.e. contains arbitrarily large numbers.","The domain is the whole line: $X=\\mathbb{R}$.","$f$ is bounded on its whole domain $X$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ea9048e7aee09a6c40827c1b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_007_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $(M_0,+\\infty)\\subseteq X$. Which statement means $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}f(x)=L$?","options":["For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $M\\ge M_0$ with $|f(x)-L|<\\varepsilon$ for all $x>M$.","There is $M\\ge M_0$ with $|f(x)-L|<\\varepsilon$ for all $x>M$ and all $\\varepsilon>0$.","For every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is some $x>M_0$ with $|f(x)-L|<\\varepsilon$.","For every $M\\ge M_0$ there is $\\varepsilon>0$ with $|f(x)-L|<\\varepsilon$ for all $x>M$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"54e243469d5d4f9a0bb416dc","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_008_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty}f(x) = L$, which implication is required?","options":["$x \\gt M\\implies |f(x)-L| \\lt \\varepsilon$","$0 \\lt |x-M| \\lt \\varepsilon$","$f(M) = L$","$x \\lt M\\implies |f(x)-L| \\lt \\varepsilon$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b78b92c0fd602359a1b07262","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=3+\\frac{2}{x}$ for $x>0$ and let $\\varepsilon=0.1$. What is the smallest $M\\ge0$ such that every $x>M$ satisfies $|f(x)-3|<\\varepsilon$?","options":["$10$","$20$","$0.2$","No such $M$ exists, since $f(x)\\neq3$ for every $x>0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3122a8a973864f8f4494f645","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_limit_at_infinity_92aa3114_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which function $f$ satisfies $\\lim_{x\\to+\\infty}f(x)=+\\infty$?","options":["$f(x)=\\ln x$ for $x>0$","$f(x)=x\\sin x$ for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$f(x)=\\frac{x}{x+1}$ for $x>0$","$f(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}$ for $x>1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"00da0f64473bb5836d122342","time_limit":30}]}}